---
// stead.ing's front page. The hero's words come from content/site.yaml; the
// rest of the story is written here.
import type { Landing } from "../lib/schema.ts";
import { PROJECTS } from "../projects.ts";
import ProductMap from "./ProductMap.astro";
import ProjectCard from "./ProjectCard.astro";
import PromptBox from "./PromptBox.astro";

interface Props {
  landing: Landing;
}

const { landing } = Astro.props;
const section = "mt-16 space-y-4";
const reading = "max-w-prose space-y-4";
---

<section class="space-y-6">
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">{landing.headline}</h1>
  <p class="max-w-prose text-xl">{landing.summary}</p>
  <div class="max-w-prose">
    <PromptBox prompt={landing.prompt} />
  </div>
  <p><a href="#projects">Browse the projects</a></p>
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="pain">
  <h2 id="pain" class="text-2xl font-semibold">You've got more ideas than hours.</h2>
  <div class={reading}>
    <p>
      You pay for a model subscription and never come close to using it up. Not for lack of ideas: every idea turns into
      something you have to babysit. Approve a tool call. Answer a question. Switch windows. Lose the thread. Every morning is
      Monday brain.
    </p>
    <p>
      So you build tools to fix it, and every tool becomes one more thing to patch, deploy, sandbox, and secure. Stop building
      and nothing changes. Keep building and the upkeep buries you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shortcut everyone's taking (a spare Mac, an agent with free rein, every secret you own) works right up until one
      prompt injection loses you the farm.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hard part is the plan. Dream big, plan hard, and let your agents break it into tasks that know what depends on what.
      After that, it's a loop. Steading is that loop, with the fences, the upkeep, and the proof already built in, so you stop
      pushing buttons and start building what you actually wanted.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="idea">
  <h2 id="idea" class="text-2xl font-semibold">A platform you can take apart</h2>
  <p class="max-w-prose">
    Everyone else is building one big system for you to move into. Steading is a platform too, but it's made of projects, each
    built to do one job well, each with an open interface. Start with the project that fixes today's problem, and add another
    when you need it. If you have a better idea for a project, swap in your own; the rest keeps working.
  </p>
  <ProductMap />
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="projects">
  <h2 id="projects" class="text-2xl font-semibold">What's inside</h2>
  <ul class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
    {PROJECTS.map((project) => <ProjectCard project={project} />)}
  </ul>
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="proof">
  <h2 id="proof" class="text-2xl font-semibold">Proof</h2>
  <div class={reading}>
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">One line, tens of cents.</h3>
    <p>
      <code>@types/node</code> 26 broke a build in{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/ivy/thicket/pull/159"><code>ivy/thicket#159</code></a>. Nobody was going to look at it.
      Almanac had an agent fix one line in a sandbox with no network. Plain code checked the patch, Almanac pushed it, and CI
      passed.
    </p>
    <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-mono text-sm" tabindex="0" aria-label="The repair, as a diff"><code>-      body: new Blob([bytes]),
+      body: new Blob([new Uint8Array(bytes)]),</code></pre>
    <h3 class="pt-4 text-lg font-semibold">Steading builds Steading.</h3>
    <p>
      Skift is built from <a href="https://github.com/steading-ai/skift/blob/main/backlog.toml">its own backlog</a>: every
      task a work order an agent picks up with an empty context window.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="beliefs">
  <h2 id="beliefs" class="text-2xl font-semibold">What we believe</h2>
  <ul class="max-w-prose space-y-3">
    <li><strong>You decide what. Agents do the rest.</strong> Nobody should have to sit in a chat window for work to move.</li>
    <li><strong>Proof, not promises.</strong> "Done" means a program checked it.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real fences, not polite requests.</strong> What an agent can touch is limited by the operating system, the
      network, or a virtual machine, not by a sentence in a prompt.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Code over prompts.</strong> A model is called only where judgment is actually needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Yours.</strong> Your hardware, your database, your rules.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="open">
  <h2 id="open" class="text-2xl font-semibold">Open for good</h2>
  <div class={reading}>
    <p>
      Steading is open source, permanently. Contributors keep their copyright, so nobody, including us, can change the license
      later. <a href="/open-for-good/">How we keep that promise</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class={section} aria-labelledby="join">
  <h2 id="join" class="text-2xl font-semibold">Don't build it all yourself.</h2>
  <div class={reading}>
    <p>
      Steading is built by a crew of friends, and there's room for more. Pick up an open task, or bring a project of your own
      and keep your name on it. <a href="/how-we-work/">How we work, and how a project joins</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/steading-ai">Steading on GitHub</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
